WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.189 --> 00:00:02.730
RETO MEIER: Today I learned that
since 2010, New Yorkers have

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.130
been complaining more.

00:00:04.130 --> 00:00:05.880
I'm Reto Meier, Google
Developer Advocate.

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.100
Join me now as I explore
the trends from the past six

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:10.850
years of New York City
311 calls with BigQuery.

00:00:10.850 --> 00:00:13.544
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.169 --> 00:00:17.460
RETO MEIER: Most complaint
types have seen an increase,

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.590
but they are not
consistently distributed,

00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:22.530
and many categories have
actually seen a decrease.

00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:24.420
The most significant
complaint increases

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.820
are related to cars,
noises, and garbage.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.700
The most striking are those
related to parked cars.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.630
Frankly, I'm amazed anyone even
tries to park in New York City.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:36.630
Illegal parking complaints
are up over 400%,

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:40.170
for presenting nearly
100,000 additional calls.

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:42.000
That, by itself,
is almost a third

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:44.550
of the total
increase since 2010.

00:00:44.550 --> 00:00:46.210
Blocked driveways
and derelict vehicles

00:00:46.210 --> 00:00:48.630
represent another
100,000 extra calls.

00:00:48.630 --> 00:00:50.160
So it's clear that
people just don't

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.680
know where to leave their cars.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.952
Note that there is also a sharp
increase in broken parking

00:00:55.952 --> 00:00:56.660
meter complaints.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:01:00.700
So some people, at least, are
trying to do the right thing.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.830
Noise complaints have always
accounted for the most 311

00:01:03.830 --> 00:01:04.330
calls.

00:01:04.330 --> 00:01:06.520
They have now more than
doubled since 2010,

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:11.140
with over 200,000 additional
calls in 2016, which represents

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.840
nearly 18% of all calls.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.850
There's also notable
increase in complaints

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.790
about homeless encampments,
dirty sidewalks, and missed

00:01:18.790 --> 00:01:22.690
garbage pickups, and a possibly
not unrelated increase in rat

00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:24.130
sightings.

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.950
Thankfully, the trend hasn't
been entirely one way,

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:30.075
with many complaint types
seeing significant decreases.

00:01:30.075 --> 00:01:32.200
The city walls are looking
cleaner, with complaints

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.940
about graffiti and
illegal postering

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:36.610
both down, and illegal
postering, specifically,

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:38.460
down nearly 80%.

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.660
Broken streetlights and
faulty traffic signals

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:43.000
are both down
around 50%, possibly

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.630
related to the gradual
introduction of LEDs,

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:49.430
and street repairs seem to
be failing far less often.

00:01:49.430 --> 00:01:50.950
We can also see
that each borough

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:52.747
has evolved in its own way.

00:01:52.747 --> 00:01:54.580
Here I'm looking at the
complaint types that

00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:57.770
have increased or decreased at
significantly different rates

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:00.410
in one borough,
compared to the other.

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:02.970
In Brooklyn, for example,
derelict vehicle complaints

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:06.350
are more than 60% higher
than the other boroughs.

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.660
Manhattan saw the largest
growth in complaints,

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:11.480
and as you can see, really
drove some of the categories

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.400
with the biggest
increases, particularly

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.430
for noise and illegal parking.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:18.230
They also went against the trend
for graffiti, street cave-ins,

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:20.810
and faulty pedestrian
signals, getting worse

00:02:20.810 --> 00:02:23.780
where the other boroughs
all saw an improvement.

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:25.850
In the Bronx, complaints
about loud music,

00:02:25.850 --> 00:02:27.590
on the street and
coming from cars,

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:30.287
have grown at around twice the
rate of the other boroughs.

00:02:30.287 --> 00:02:32.870
Meanwhile, in Queens, complaints
about poor street conditions,

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:35.900
including potholes and
cracked roads, are up 10%,

00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:39.030
against a 5% downward
trend everywhere else.

00:02:39.030 --> 00:02:41.510
Finally, in Staten Island,
the alleys are even dirtier

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:44.370
and broken street lights
are up against the trend.

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:45.800
Remember that in
every case here,

00:02:45.800 --> 00:02:48.657
we're measuring the change
in the number of complaints.

00:02:48.657 --> 00:02:50.240
You'd expect an
increase in complaints

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.610
to correlate with more
problems, but it's possible

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:54.350
that people are just
complaining more.

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:55.400
I've cherry-picked
what I thought

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:56.774
were the most
interesting trends,

00:02:56.774 --> 00:02:59.210
but they are by no
means comprehensive.

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.910
As always, your first terabyte
of processing each month

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:04.640
is free, so I invite you
to open up BigQuery and do

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.560
your own trend
investigations on this

00:03:06.560 --> 00:03:08.660
and all the other
public data sets.

